1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,696
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,757 --> 00:00:10,087
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:10,087 --> 00:00:10,813
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,874 --> 00:00:15,993
Welcome to Unit 4: The Structure of the Atom and the Periodic Table.

5
00:00:17,054 --> 00:00:23,475
Hydrogen fuel burns cleanly, releasing only water vapour and heat, so it could help replace fossil fuels.

6
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:29,654
The uses of each element depend on its properties. So how do scientists study elements systematically?

7
00:00:30,714 --> 00:00:36,833
Scientists once thought atoms were like a plum pudding: positive and negative charges spread evenly.

8
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:40,710
Then Ernest Rutherford fired alpha particles at thin gold foil.

9
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:45,677
Most passed through, but some bounced back! Atoms must have a tiny, dense nucleus.

10
00:00:46,738 --> 00:00:51,281
Today's model: the nucleus contains positive protons and neutral neutrons,

11
00:00:51,281 --> 00:00:56,309
and negative electrons orbit around it in shells. Most of the atom is empty space.

12
00:00:56,309 --> 00:01:01,215
An atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons, so it's electrically neutral.

13
00:01:02,276 --> 00:01:07,789
Every element's use depends on its properties, which come from the structure of its atoms.

14
00:01:07,789 --> 00:01:09,182
Here are four examples.

15
00:01:10,242 --> 00:01:16,361
Helium is much less dense than air and does not react, so it is used safely in balloons and airships.

16
00:01:17,421 --> 00:01:21,904
Lithium is the lightest metal and gives up its one outer electron easily,

17
00:01:21,904 --> 00:01:25,963
which makes it ideal for rechargeable batteries in phones and cars.

18
00:01:27,023 --> 00:01:33,444
Silicon conducts electricity only a little, so it is used to make the tiny switches inside computer chips.

19
00:01:34,505 --> 00:01:40,563
Hydrogen burns with oxygen to make only water, so it can be used as a clean fuel for buses and cars.

20
00:01:41,623 --> 00:01:47,620
In each case, the structure of the atom decides the properties, and the properties decide the use.

21
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,467
Pause and think of another element and its use.

22
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:56,677
There are over 100 elements, each shown on the Periodic Table with its symbol, name,

23
00:01:56,677 --> 00:01:58,494
atomic number and mass number.

24
00:01:59,555 --> 00:02:04,522
The atomic number is the number of protons, which equals the number of electrons.

25
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:10,156
The mass number is protons plus neutrons, together called nucleons. Carbon: atomic number 6,

26
00:02:10,156 --> 00:02:15,305
mass number 12, so it has 6 protons, 6 electrons and 12 minus 6, which is 6 neutrons.

27
00:02:16,366 --> 00:02:20,303
Atoms are so tiny that we need electron microscopes to see them.

28
00:02:20,303 --> 00:02:23,271
These scan a beam of electrons across a surface,

29
00:02:23,271 --> 00:02:27,512
and a transmission electron microscope can even show individual atoms.

30
00:02:28,572 --> 00:02:33,782
Electrons move around the nucleus in shells. The first shell holds up to 2 electrons.

31
00:02:33,782 --> 00:02:37,295
The second holds up to 8, and the third is stable with 8.

32
00:02:37,295 --> 00:02:40,506
We write the arrangement as the electronic structure.

33
00:02:41,566 --> 00:02:47,140
Carbon has 6 electrons: 2 in the first shell and 4 in the second, so its structure is 2, 4.

34
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,320
Nitrogen, with 7 electrons, is 2, 5.

35
00:02:50,381 --> 00:02:54,318
Magnesium has 12 electrons: 2, 8, 2. Phosphorus has 15: 2, 8, 5.

36
00:02:54,318 --> 00:02:59,104
Notice the last number: it tells you how many electrons are in the outer shell.
